! o-chain-afm-us.in with both moments turned into the xy plane and computed by
! the noncollinear path instead of the collinear one. Same cell, same atoms,
! same k-grid, same antiferromagnetic state: without spin-orbit coupling the
! energy cannot know which axis the moments lie on, so the two totals are one
! number computed by two of this code's SCF paths.
!
! **It is a control, and it exists because an identity that closes is not
! evidence.** P95 validates an augmented spin spiral at q = b3/2 against
! o-chain-afm-us.in, and the residue that identity left was explained as a
! G-sphere truncation the spiral causes. Running this pair -- the same doubled
! cell with no spiral anywhere in it -- showed the residue is twice the spiral's
! at every cutoff, the factor a doubled cell's energy is divided by, so the
! identity was measuring the gap between these two paths and the spiral was
! along for the ride.
!
! pw.x has both paths too, and reproduces the gap: see OPEN.md Y4.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 6, celldm(1) = 12.0, celldm(3) = 0.86666666666667,
    nat = 2, ntyp = 2,
    ecutwfc = 25.0, ecutrho = 200.0,
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'gaussian', degauss = 0.10
    noncolin = .true.
    nosym = .true.
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.6, angle1(1) = 90.0, angle2(1) =   0.0
    starting_magnetization(2) = 0.6, angle1(2) = 90.0, angle2(2) = 180.0
    nbnd = 40
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 O1  16.0  O.pz-rrkjus.UPF
 O2  16.0  O.pz-rrkjus.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 O1 0.0 0.0 0.0
 O2 0.0 0.0 0.5
K_POINTS {automatic}
 1 1 2 0 0 0
